IMPORT prelude

-- Magistrates Court Act 2004 (Western Australia) -- the Court that hears my claim: who may sit, where, on what
-- jurisdiction, and what this Act itself gives a party.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant ("May the Magistrates Court hear my claim? Who will hear it, and where? Which rules of evidence
-- apply? If a registrar refuses my claim or decides against me, what can I do? What can I see of the Court's record of my
-- case?"). Every exported question is phrased as that person would ask it. Where the Act speaks to the Court, a
-- magistrate, a registrar or the Chief Magistrate, it is encoded as what that means for the claimant.
--
-- THE SPLIT WITH THE OTHER BASELINE. This Act constitutes the Court and points outward for its jurisdiction: s 9 ("the
-- jurisdiction conferred on it by this Act and by other written laws") and s 10 ("The Court's civil jurisdiction is set
-- out in the Magistrates Court (Civil Proceedings) Act 2004"). The money limits, the excluded claims, the consumer/trader
-- claim and the minor cases procedure are encoded in that Act's baseline
-- (../../../magistrates-court-civil-proceedings-act-2004/encodings/baseline-2026-10/mccpa-minor-cases.l4) and are NOT
-- repeated here: whether the conferring law reaches the claim is an input to this module.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 3 (Court officer, JP, registrar, as glosses); s 4(2) (a court of record, noted); s 7(1), (2), (4),
-- (6) (who constitutes the Court; a bench of JPs); s 8(1)(c), (2) (where the Court may sit); s 9, s 10 (where the Court's
-- jurisdiction comes from); s 14 (rules of evidence); s 16(4) (the most a person can be punished for contempt); s 17(2),
-- (3), (6), (7) (a document refused as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse of process); s 23(1) (correction of accidental
-- errors); s 28(1)(b), (c) (what a registrar may not do); s 29 (appeal from a registrar to a magistrate, 21 days); s 30
-- (the Court's duty to a self-represented party); s 33(3), (4), (8), (11)(a) (a party's access to the Court's records).
--
-- Edition: the Magistrates Court Act 2004 (No. 47 of 2004), compilation as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_47672.txt (legislation.wa.gov.au mrdoc 47672, retrieved 2026-09-07); its s 11A
-- carries No. 10 of 2023. No regulations or rules of court are used: neither the Magistrates Court Regulations nor the
-- Magistrates Court (General) Rules are deposited, so the circumstances in which JPs may constitute the Court (s 7(2)) and
-- what the rules of court delegate to a registrar (s 28(1)) are inputs. "Written law" in s 9 is read with the
-- Interpretation Act 1984 s 5, as deposited at ../../../interpretation-act-1984/registers/source-bundle/.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: s 4A (electronic processes); ss 5, 6 and Schedules 1-3 (appointment, tenure and functions of magistrates);
-- s 7(3), (5); s 8(1)(a), (b), (3), (5) (registries, sitting times, chambers); s 11 and s 11A (criminal jurisdiction: not
-- the claimant's); s 12, s 13 (noted in NOTES.md); s 15 and s 16 beyond (4) (what is a contempt, and the procedure);
-- ss 18-22 (oaths, affidavits, warrants, cancellation, inspection); s 23(2), (3); s 28(1)(a), (2); s 33(1), (2), (5)-(7),
-- (9), (10), (11)(b), (12) (criminal records, fees, the rules); ss 31, 32, 34-37 (reasons, effect of process,
-- presumptions, review orders by the Supreme Court, immunities); Part 4 save ss 28, 29 (administration); ss 38-42 (practice
-- directions, rules of court, regulations, fees).
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the seat of the claimant, with the design defaults
-- ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as
-- advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Magistrates Court Act 2004 -- the claimant`

§§ `My case -- the facts`

-- s 9: "The Court has the jurisdiction conferred on it by this Act and by other written laws."
-- s 10: "The Court's civil jurisdiction is set out in the Magistrates Court (Civil Proceedings) Act 2004."
-- Interpretation Act 1984 s 5: "written law means all Acts for the time being in force and all subsidiary legislation for
-- the time being in force", and "Act" means an Act of the Parliament of Western Australia. A Commonwealth Act is not a
-- "written law" in that sense.
DECLARE `Where my claim's jurisdiction comes from` IS ONE OF
    `an ordinary civil claim, such as a debt or damages`                         -- s 10: the Civil Proceedings Act
    `a Western Australian written law that gives the Court the jurisdiction`    -- s 9: "other written laws"
    `a Commonwealth law that gives a magistrates court the jurisdiction`        -- not a "written law"; see below
    `no law that I can point to`

DECLARE `My case` HAS
    `where my claim's jurisdiction comes from`                                   IS A `Where my claim's jurisdiction comes from`
    -- Answered by the conferring law, not by this Act: for an ordinary civil claim, the Civil Proceedings Act baseline's
    -- `Can the Magistrates Court hear my claim`.
    `that law gives the Court jurisdiction over my claim`                         IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 7(2), (4): facts about how the Court must or may be constituted
    `another written law requires the Court to be constituted in a particular way for it`   IS A BOOLEAN
    `this step is only an adjournment or another procedural decision`            IS A BOOLEAN
    `the regulations or another written law let JPs constitute the Court for it`  IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 8(1)(c), (2): where the Court is to sit
    `the Court is to sit in Western Australia`                                    IS A BOOLEAN
    `the Chief Magistrate has approved the sitting outside the State`             IS A BOOLEAN
    `the law of that place allows the Court to sit there`                         IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 14: "Subject to any other written law"
    `another law displaces the Supreme Court rules of evidence for my claim`      IS A BOOLEAN
    -- s 30
    `I am representing myself`                                                    IS A BOOLEAN

§§ `ss 9, 10 -- may the Court hear my claim`

-- s 9 and s 10, read together: the Court has no jurisdiction of its own beyond what this Act and other written laws
-- confer, and this Act confers no civil jurisdiction itself; it sends the reader to the Civil Proceedings Act.
-- A Commonwealth law (for example the Fair Work Act 2009 s 548, small claims "from a magistrates court") is not a
-- "written law" (Interpretation Act s 5). INFERENCE, not read in the deposit: such a law confers jurisdiction of its own
-- force on a State court, so the answer is yes when it reaches the claim, but not because of s 9. The answer says so.
DECLARE `Whether the Court can hear my claim` IS ONE OF
    `yes: under the Magistrates Court (Civil Proceedings) Act 2004, as s 10 says`
    `yes: under the written law that confers the jurisdiction, as s 9 says`
    `yes: under the Commonwealth law that confers it, which s 9 does not itself name`
    `no: the law my claim rests on does not give the Court jurisdiction over it`
    `no: the Court has only the jurisdiction that a law confers on it (s 9)`

@export May the Magistrates Court hear my claim, and under which law (ss 9, 10)?
GIVEN `the case` IS A `My case` @desc Where your claim's jurisdiction comes from, and whether that law reaches your claim
GIVETH A `Whether the Court can hear my claim`
DECIDE `May the Magistrates Court hear my claim` IS
    CONSIDER `the case`'s `where my claim's jurisdiction comes from`
        WHEN `no law that I can point to`
            THEN `no: the Court has only the jurisdiction that a law confers on it (s 9)`
        WHEN `an ordinary civil claim, such as a debt or damages`
            THEN IF `the case`'s `that law gives the Court jurisdiction over my claim`
                 THEN `yes: under the Magistrates Court (Civil Proceedings) Act 2004, as s 10 says`
                 ELSE `no: the law my claim rests on does not give the Court jurisdiction over it`
        WHEN `a Western Australian written law that gives the Court the jurisdiction`
            THEN IF `the case`'s `that law gives the Court jurisdiction over my claim`
                 THEN `yes: under the written law that confers the jurisdiction, as s 9 says`
                 ELSE `no: the law my claim rests on does not give the Court jurisdiction over it`
        WHEN `a Commonwealth law that gives a magistrates court the jurisdiction`
            THEN IF `the case`'s `that law gives the Court jurisdiction over my claim`
                 THEN `yes: under the Commonwealth law that confers it, which s 9 does not itself name`
                 ELSE `no: the law my claim rests on does not give the Court jurisdiction over it`

§§ `s 7 -- who will hear my case`

-- s 7(1): "The Court is to be constituted by one magistrate."
-- s 7(2): "In circumstances prescribed by the regulations or by another written law, the Court may be constituted by --
-- (a) 2 or more JPs; or (b) one JP."
-- s 7(4): "If another written law requires the Court to be constituted in a particular way to deal with a case, it must be
-- constituted in that way despite subsections (1) and (2), except for the purposes of adjourning the case or making other
-- procedural decisions in relation to the case."
-- Gloss: JP is a Justice of the Peace (s 3), a lay justice, not a lawyer magistrate. The regulations are not deposited:
-- whether they allow JPs for this step is an input.
DECLARE `Who may hear my case` IS ONE OF
    `one magistrate (s 7(1))`
    `one magistrate, or one or more JPs, as the regulations or another written law allow (s 7(2))`
    `the Court as constituted in the way the other written law requires (s 7(4))`

@export Who may constitute the Court to hear my case (s 7(1), (2), (4))?
GIVEN `the case` IS A `My case` @desc Whether another law fixes how the Court is made up for your case, whether this step is only procedural, and whether JPs are allowed
GIVETH A `Who may hear my case`
DECIDE `Who may constitute the Court for my case` IS
    BRANCH
        IF      `the case`'s `another written law requires the Court to be constituted in a particular way for it`
            AND (NOT (`the case`'s `this step is only an adjournment or another procedural decision`))
            THEN `the Court as constituted in the way the other written law requires (s 7(4))`
        IF `the case`'s `the regulations or another written law let JPs constitute the Court for it`
            THEN `one magistrate, or one or more JPs, as the regulations or another written law allow (s 7(2))`
        OTHERWISE `one magistrate (s 7(1))`

-- s 7(6): "If the Court is constituted by 2 or more JPs for the purpose of dealing with a case -- (a) all of them must be
-- present at all times when dealing with the case; and (b) the decision of the Court is the decision of the majority of
-- them; but if they are equally divided, the case is to be adjourned to a place and date decided by them or, if they
-- cannot agree, by the one who was first appointed as a JP".
DECLARE `What a bench of JPs decides` IS ONE OF
    `s 7(6) does not apply: a single JP decides alone`
    `no valid decision: every JP must be present throughout (s 7(6)(a))`
    `decided in my favour by the majority (s 7(6)(b))`
    `decided against me by the majority (s 7(6)(b))`
    `adjourned to a place and date the JPs decide, the JPs being equally divided (s 7(6)(b))`

@export If a bench of JPs hears my case, what is the result of their vote (s 7(6))?
GIVEN `JPs for me` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the JPs decide in your favour
      `JPs against me` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of the JPs decide against you
      `every JP was present throughout` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all of the JPs were present at all times while dealing with your case
GIVETH A `What a bench of JPs decides`
DECIDE `What does the bench of JPs decide` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `JPs for me` PLUS `JPs against me` LESS THAN 2
            THEN `s 7(6) does not apply: a single JP decides alone`
        IF (NOT `every JP was present throughout`)
            THEN `no valid decision: every JP must be present throughout (s 7(6)(a))`
        IF `JPs for me` GREATER THAN `JPs against me`
            THEN `decided in my favour by the majority (s 7(6)(b))`
        IF `JPs against me` GREATER THAN `JPs for me`
            THEN `decided against me by the majority (s 7(6)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `adjourned to a place and date the JPs decide, the JPs being equally divided (s 7(6)(b))`

§§ `s 8 -- where my case may be heard`

-- s 8(1)(c): the Court "may, despite paragraphs (a) and (b), sit or otherwise exercise its jurisdiction at any time and
-- place, but must not do so at a place outside the State without the approval of the Chief Magistrate."
-- s 8(2): nothing authorises sitting "at a place outside the State except to the extent allowed by the law of the
-- jurisdiction in which the place is located."
@export May the Court sit at that place to deal with my case (s 8(1)(c), (2))?
GIVEN `the case` IS A `My case` @desc Whether the sitting is in Western Australia and, if not, whether the Chief Magistrate approved it and the law there allows it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Court sit there for my case` IF
        `the case`'s `the Court is to sit in Western Australia`
    OR  (    `the case`'s `the Chief Magistrate has approved the sitting outside the State`
         AND `the case`'s `the law of that place allows the Court to sit there`)

§§ `s 14 -- the rules of evidence`

-- s 14: "Subject to any other written law, the rules of evidence that apply in the Supreme Court apply in the Magistrates
-- Court." Another law that displaces them (for example the Civil Proceedings Act s 29(4) for a minor case, or the Fair
-- Work Act 2009 s 548(3) for small claims proceedings) is asked as a fact. Reading a Commonwealth law as displacing them is
-- an INFERENCE: s 14 names only "written law".
@export Do the Supreme Court rules of evidence apply to my case (s 14)?
GIVEN `the case` IS A `My case` @desc Whether another law says the Court is not bound by the rules of evidence for your claim
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do the Supreme Court rules of evidence apply to my case` IF
    NOT (`the case`'s `another law displaces the Supreme Court rules of evidence for my claim`)

§§ `s 30 -- if I represent myself`

-- s 30: "In a case where a party is selfrepresented, the Court must inform the party of -- (a) the need, when
-- crossexamining a witness called by another party, to ask the witness about any evidence of which the witness or the
-- other party has not previously had notice that the selfrepresented party -- (i) intends to adduce; and (ii) intends to
-- allege will contradict the witness's evidence; and (b) the consequences of not doing so."
-- Gloss: if you will call evidence that contradicts a witness, you must put it to that witness first, and the Court must
-- tell you so.
@export Must the Court tell me that I have to put my contradicting evidence to the other side's witnesses (s 30)?
GIVEN `the case` IS A `My case` @desc Whether you are representing yourself
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the Court explain cross-examination to me` IF
    `the case`'s `I am representing myself`

§§ `s 17 -- if the registry refuses my claim`

-- s 17(1): "document includes a claim, prosecution notice, application, and affidavit."
-- s 17(2): if it "appears to a registrar that a document lodged with the Court is an abuse of the Court's process or is
-- frivolous or vexatious, the registrar may refuse to accept the document unless a magistrate has given leave".
-- s 17(3): "A person affected by such a refusal may apply to a magistrate for leave to lodge the document." (6): the
-- magistrate "may give leave and may do so on terms or may refuse leave" -- a judgment, asked as an input.
-- s 17(7): if the document was "lodged within a time limit" and leave is given later, "it may be lodged pursuant to that
-- leave even if the time limit has by then expired."
@export After the registrar refused my claim as frivolous, vexatious or an abuse of process, may I lodge it (s 17)?
GIVEN `a magistrate gave leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a magistrate has given you leave to lodge it
      `I first lodged it within the time limit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you first lodged it before any time limit for it ran out
      `the time limit has now expired` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the time limit for lodging it has run out by now
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I lodge my refused claim` IF
        `a magistrate gave leave`
    AND (   (NOT `the time limit has now expired`)
         OR `I first lodged it within the time limit`)

§§ `ss 28, 29 -- registrars`

-- s 28(1): the rules of court "may delegate to a registrar any or all of the Court's jurisdiction and powers other than the
-- power -- ... (b) in the exercise of the Court's civil jurisdiction, to enter a final judgment on a case after trial; or
-- (c) to find a person guilty of a contempt of the Court." What the rules delegate is an input (not deposited).
-- Gloss: a registrar is a court official (Principal Registrar, registrar or deputy registrar, s 3), not a magistrate.
DECLARE `A step in my case` IS ONE OF
    `entering final judgment on my claim after trial`
    `finding a person guilty of contempt of the Court`
    `another step that the rules of court delegate to a registrar`
    `another step that the rules of court do not delegate`

@export May a registrar, rather than a magistrate, take this step in my case (s 28(1))?
GIVEN `the step` IS A `A step in my case` @desc The step to be taken in your case
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May a registrar take this step in my case` IF
    `the step` EQUALS `another step that the rules of court delegate to a registrar`

-- s 29(1): "A person dissatisfied by a decision made by a registrar in the exercise of any of the Court's jurisdiction or
-- powers delegated to the registrar under section 28 may appeal to a magistrate."
-- s 29(2): "The appeal must be commenced within 21 days after the date of the registrar's decision". (3): "A magistrate may
-- extend the period ... even if it has elapsed" -- a judgment, asked as an input. (4): "by way of a new hearing".
@export How many days do I have to appeal a registrar's decision to a magistrate (s 29(2))?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `How many days to appeal a registrar's decision` IS 21

@export Can I appeal the registrar's decision in my case to a magistrate (s 29)?
GIVEN `it was made under a power delegated by s 28` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the registrar decided in exercise of the Court's jurisdiction or powers delegated under s 28
      `days since the decision` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days after the date of the registrar's decision you would start the appeal
      `a magistrate extended the time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a magistrate has extended the time for you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can I appeal the registrar's decision` IF
        `it was made under a power delegated by s 28`
    AND (   `days since the decision` AT MOST `How many days to appeal a registrar's decision`
         OR `a magistrate extended the time`)

@export Will my appeal from a registrar be heard afresh (s 29(4))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my appeal from a registrar a new hearing` IS TRUE

§§ `s 23 -- mistakes in my judgment`

-- s 23(1): "The Court may correct a judgment or order if it contains -- (a) an accidental slip or omission; or (b) a material
-- arithmetic error; or (c) a material mistake in the description of any person, thing or matter." (2): on the application
-- of "a person affected". A different view of the merits is not a slip; that is for an appeal.
DECLARE `The mistake in my judgment` IS ONE OF
    `an accidental slip or omission`
    `a material arithmetic error`
    `a material mistake in describing a person, thing or matter`
    `a wrong decision on the facts or the law`

@export May the Court correct this mistake in the judgment in my case (s 23(1))?
GIVEN `the mistake` IS A `The mistake in my judgment` @desc What is wrong with the judgment or order
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the Court correct my judgment` IF
    (NOT (`the mistake` EQUALS `a wrong decision on the facts or the law`))

§§ `s 33 -- what I can see of the Court's record of my case`

-- s 33(3): "A party to a case is entitled, on request, to inspect or obtain a copy of the following documents -- (a) any
-- document that has been lodged with or issued by the Court as required by law and that forms part of the Court's records
-- of the case; (b) a copy of any document admitted as evidence ...; (c) if an electronic recording has been made of the
-- proceedings and a transcript of it has been prepared, a copy of the transcript; (d) if no such recording was made, a copy
-- of the record of proceedings made by the person or persons constituting the Court; (e) any written judgment (including
-- the reasons for it) given, or written order made".
-- s 33(4): "With the leave of the Court, a party to a case may -- (a) listen to or view ... any electronic recording ...;
-- (b) inspect or obtain a copy of any document held by the Court in relation to the case".
-- s 33(8): on an application by a person, the Court "unless it has good reason not to do so, shall give the person leave".
-- s 33(11)(a): nothing requires "the person constituting the Court make available any note made for their own purposes".
DECLARE `What I want from the record` IS ONE OF
    `a document lodged with or issued by the Court that is part of its record of my case`
    `a document admitted in evidence`
    `the transcript of the electronic recording of the hearing`
    `the record of proceedings made by the magistrate or JPs`
    `a written judgment, its reasons, or a written order`
    `an electronic recording of the hearing, or one tendered in it`
    `another document the Court holds about my case`
    `a note the magistrate made for their own purposes`

DECLARE `My access` IS ONE OF
    `yes, as of right on request (s 33(3))`
    `only with the Court's leave (s 33(4))`
    `only with leave, which the Court must give unless it has good reason not to (s 33(8))`
    `no: nothing requires it to be made available (s 33(11)(a))`

@export Can I see or get a copy of this from the Court's record of my case (s 33)?
GIVEN `the thing` IS A `What I want from the record` @desc What you want to inspect, copy, see or hear
      `I am a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the case
      `the hearing was electronically recorded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an electronic recording was made of the proceedings
      `a transcript was prepared` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a transcript of that recording has been prepared
GIVETH A `My access`
DECIDE `Can I get this from the record of my case` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the thing` EQUALS `a note the magistrate made for their own purposes`
            THEN `no: nothing requires it to be made available (s 33(11)(a))`
        IF (NOT `I am a party`)
            THEN `only with leave, which the Court must give unless it has good reason not to (s 33(8))`
        IF `the thing` EQUALS `the transcript of the electronic recording of the hearing`
            THEN IF `the hearing was electronically recorded` AND `a transcript was prepared`
                 THEN `yes, as of right on request (s 33(3))`
                 ELSE `only with the Court's leave (s 33(4))`
        IF `the thing` EQUALS `the record of proceedings made by the magistrate or JPs`
            THEN IF (NOT `the hearing was electronically recorded`)
                 THEN `yes, as of right on request (s 33(3))`
                 ELSE `only with the Court's leave (s 33(4))`
        IF     `the thing` EQUALS `an electronic recording of the hearing, or one tendered in it`
            OR `the thing` EQUALS `another document the Court holds about my case`
            THEN `only with the Court's leave (s 33(4))`
        OTHERWISE `yes, as of right on request (s 33(3))`

§§ `s 16(4) -- contempt`

-- s 16(4): "A person guilty of a contempt is liable to a fine of not more than $12 000 or imprisonment for not more than
-- 12 months or both." s 15(2) makes it a contempt for a summoned witness or a party required to produce a record not to
-- do so "without reasonable excuse"; what is a contempt is not encoded.
@export What is the most I can be fined for a contempt of the Magistrates Court (s 16(4)), in dollars?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most I can be fined for contempt` IS 12000

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for a contempt of the Magistrates Court (s 16(4)), in months?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest I can be imprisoned for contempt in months` IS 12
